course: biology
lesson: 284
title: Conservation heroes: Attenborough, Goodall, Carson, Maathai and the rangers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every comeback story has people behind it: scientists who watched animals for years, writers who warned the world, and rangers who guard animals day and night.
explain: Jane Goodall sat quietly in a Tanzanian forest from 1960 until the chimpanzees trusted her, and she saw something nobody believed: a chimp stripping leaves from a twig to fish termites out of a mound, so chimps make tools.
explain: Rachel Carson wrote a book, Silent Spring, about how pesticides harmed birds, and it helped get DDT banned in America, which is where the bald eagle's comeback began.
explain: Heroes come in every size: a village woman planting a tree in Kenya, a nine-year-old boy in Germany starting a tree campaign, or you counting birds in your garden for scientists.
fact: David Attenborough has made nature films for over 70 years, showing millions of people the wonders of nature and the dangers to it; Blue Planet II made many people think about plastic in the sea.
fact: Jane Goodall began studying chimpanzees in Tanzania in 1960 and found that chimps make and use tools, such as twigs stripped to fish for termites.
fact: Jane Goodall spent her life protecting chimps and teaching children about nature, until she died in 2025.
fact: Steve Irwin was an Australian zoo keeper and TV presenter who loved reptiles; he died in 2006 in an extremely rare stingray accident, and his family still runs Australia Zoo and saves wildlife.
fact: Dian Fossey studied mountain gorillas in Rwanda and fought against poachers who trapped them; her work helped start the protection that saved them.
fact: Wangari Maathai of Kenya started the Green Belt Movement, in which village women planted tens of millions of trees for firewood, water and soil; she won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004.
fact: Park rangers protect animals from poachers, often in danger; many are local people who know the land, and in South Africa a team of women rangers is called the Black Mambas.
fact: Dogs are trained to smell ivory and rhino horn at airports and in parks, and some find tiny rare animals for scientists; a dog's nose is thousands of times better than ours.
fact: Rachel Carson wrote Silent Spring in 1962 about how pesticides harmed birds, and the book helped lead to DDT being banned in America.
fact: Gerald Durrell founded Jersey Zoo to breed endangered animals, wrote funny books about his animal-filled childhood in Greece, and helped save animals like the pink pigeon of Mauritius.
fact: Felix Finkbeiner in Germany started a tree-planting campaign at age 9 that has planted many millions of trees; young people can lead big changes.
fact: Citizen scientists are ordinary people who count birds, butterflies and frogs and send the numbers to scientists; the Big Garden Birdwatch in Britain has hundreds of thousands of counters, and the numbers show which animals are rising or falling.
exercise: What has David Attenborough done for over 70 years? | Made nature films | Run a zoo in Jersey | Studied gorillas in Rwanda | Planted trees in Kenya
exercise: Which series made many people think about plastic in the sea? | Blue Planet II | Jurassic Park | Finding Nemo | The Lion King II
exercise: Where did Jane Goodall study chimpanzees? | Tanzania | Rwanda | Australia | Greece
exercise: What did Jane Goodall discover? | Chimps make and use tools | Chimps can talk in English | Chimps eat only bananas | Chimps cannot learn anything
exercise: What did Steve Irwin love most? | Reptiles like crocodiles | Birds of prey | Whales | Butterflies
exercise: How did Steve Irwin die? | In an extremely rare stingray accident | Bitten by a huge saltwater crocodile on his show | Stung by a jellyfish | In a car crash
exercise: Which animals did Dian Fossey study? | Mountain gorillas | Chimpanzees | Orangutans | Elephants
exercise: What did Wangari Maathai's Green Belt Movement do? | Planted tens of millions of trees | Cleaned beaches all around Britain | Bred pandas in zoos | Counted birds in gardens
exercise: Which prize did Wangari Maathai win in 2004? | The Nobel Peace Prize | An Olympic gold medal | An Oscar for a film | A football cup
exercise: What do park rangers do? | Protect animals from poachers | Sell tickets at cinemas | Feed all the animals by hand | Hunt animals for zoos
exercise: What are the Black Mambas? | A team of women rangers in South Africa | A kind of rare snake in zoos | A football team in Kenya | A pop band singing songs about saving nature
exercise: What can trained dogs sniff out at airports? | Ivory and rhino horn | Fossils inside rocks | Chocolate in suitcases for fun | Lost passports only
exercise: What was Rachel Carson's famous book? | Silent Spring | On the Origin of Species | The Jungle Book | Blue Planet
exercise: What did Rachel Carson's book help to ban in America? | The pesticide DDT | Whaling | Plastic bags | Hunting with dogs
exercise: What did Gerald Durrell found? | Jersey Zoo, to breed endangered animals | Australia Zoo, for reptiles | The Green Belt Movement in Kenya | The Big Garden Birdwatch for families
exercise: Which bird did Gerald Durrell's zoo help save? | The pink pigeon of Mauritius | The dodo | The great auk of the North Atlantic | The passenger pigeon
exercise: How old was Felix Finkbeiner when he started his tree campaign? | 9 | 19 | 29 | 4
exercise: What do citizen scientists do? | Count animals and send the numbers to scientists | Work only in big laboratories at universities | Make nature films for TV | Guard parks with rangers
test: «Steve Irwin got eaten by a croc, right?» What is right? | No, he died in an extremely rare stingray accident | Yes, a crocodile ate him | No, he was bitten by a shark while he was filming | No, he is still making shows
test: «stingrays are killers then, never going in the sea again» What is fairest? | Stingray accidents like that are extremely rare | Stingrays attack every swimmer | All stingrays chase people | Stingrays live only in the deep sea
test: «you're 10, you can't save nature, leave it to grown-ups» What does Felix Finkbeiner's story show? | He started a campaign at 9 that planted millions of trees | Only grown-ups can plant trees | Children can only watch TV about nature and learn about it | Trees planted by children do not grow
test: Count them: in a garden birdwatch you see 3 robins and 2 magpies. How many birds, legs and wings did you count? | 5 birds, 10 legs and 10 wings | 5 birds, 20 legs and 10 wings | 5 birds, 10 legs and 5 wings | 6 birds, 12 legs and 12 wings
test: What would most likely have happened to bald eagles if Silent Spring had never been written? | DDT might have stayed in use longer and harmed more eggs | Eagles would have grown bigger | Nothing could have harmed eagles | Eagles would have learnt to eat DDT and grown stronger from it
test: «a book can't save animals, it's just paper» What does Rachel Carson's story show? | Her book helped get DDT banned and started the eagle comeback | Books never change anything in the real world, only in stories | Her book was about cooking | Her book banned all birds
test: Count them: a ranger team of 4 people walks with 2 sniffer dogs. How many legs, ears and noses are there? | 16 legs, 12 ears and 6 noses | 12 legs, 12 ears and 6 noses | 16 legs, 6 ears and 6 noses | 16 legs, 12 ears and 4 noses
test: «why'd anyone believe chimps use tools, they're just animals» What did Jane Goodall see? | Chimps stripping twigs to fish termites out of mounds | Chimps building cars | Chimps writing letters on paper with pencils she gave them | Chimps cooking with fire
test: In a cartoon, a park ranger spends all day chasing a bear for its picnic basket. What do real rangers mostly do? | Protect animals and land from poachers, often in danger | Chase bears away from picnics all day | Sell sandwiches to visitors | Only drive tourists around
test: «my nan counts birds in her garden for some charity, waste of time» What is right? | Counts from many people show scientists which birds are rising or falling | It is useless, because scientists ignore any counts that come from ordinary people | Only scientists' own counts matter | Birds fly away when counted
test: «trees just for looks innit» What did women in Kenya plant trees for? | Firewood, clean water and good soil | Only to make the village look pretty | To hide from tax collectors | To feed giraffes in zoos
exam: Identify the discovery for which Jane Goodall is principally renowned. | That chimpanzees fashion and employ tools | That chimpanzees possess no capacity for learning | That chimpanzees subsist entirely upon fruit | That chimpanzees are descended from humans
exam: State the title of the work by Rachel Carson which contributed to the prohibition of DDT in the United States. | Silent Spring | On the Origin of Species | Blue Planet | The Overloaded Ark
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Wangari Maathai. | She founded the Green Belt Movement and was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004 | She founded Jersey Zoo and studied gorillas in Rwanda | She presented nature films for over seventy years | She discovered the use of tools by chimpanzees
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the activity of citizen scientists? | Members of the public record observations of wildlife and submit them to scientists | Professional scientists perform experiments in secret laboratories | Rangers pursue poachers with trained dogs | Film-makers record animals for television
